---
/**
 * Field note: "The directional-answer trap"
 * → /field-notes/directional-answer-trap/
 *
 * Its own look, and not the A/B note's either: manila spec sheet inside a
 * drafting border, IBM Plex Mono display type, clause numbering, and a title
 * block for a footer. The spine of the piece is the engineering-drawing
 * metaphor — nothing is ever perfect, which is why a real drawing states a
 * tolerance — so the design is a drawing.
 *
 * Inter is already loaded site-wide (ThemeHead.astro); only Plex Mono is
 * imported here.
 */
---

<div class="dir">
  <div class="dir-sheet">
    <div class="dir-inner">
      <!-- ============ HERO ============ -->
      <section class="dir-hero">
        <p class="dir-kicker">Field note &middot; decision quality</p>
        <h1>
          &ldquo;Give me a <span class="dir-hi">directional</span> answer.
          It doesn&rsquo;t have to be <span class="dir-strike">perfect</span>.&rdquo;
        </h1>
        <p class="dir-dek">
          One of those sentences asks for something real. The other rules
          nothing out. Both move risk from the person deciding to the person
          estimating.
        </p>
        <p class="dir-scroll" aria-hidden="true">scroll &darr;</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 1.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">1.0 &nbsp;The request</p>
        <h2>What they are actually asking for.</h2>
        <p>
          Strip the politeness and the ask is: <em>give me something fast that
          I can hang a go/no-go on.</em> They are not asking for a smaller
          answer. They want one that arrives this week and still holds up when
          the decision goes wrong.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ask deserves a serious hearing. The deadline is usually real.
          Most calls can be reversed. And an answer that lands three weeks
          after the budget is allocated is worth nothing, however good it is.
        </p>
        <p class="dir-note">
          You pay for speed with precision. That is a fair trade when both
          people know it is being made. Unsaid, it leaves you holding an
          estimate that everyone else now treats as a finding.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 2.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">2.0 &nbsp;Term one</p>
        <h2>&ldquo;Directional&rdquo; means something. Hold them to it.</h2>
        <p>
          Directional is a narrow claim, and a real one: <strong>which way,
          not how much.</strong> Up or down, better or worse, with no number
          attached.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is still a claim that can be proved wrong, and it is not free.
          You owe the answer only when the data picks a side.
        </p>

        <figure class="dir-fig">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 340 150" role="img" aria-labelledby="dir-d1">
            <title id="dir-d1"
              >Two estimates with uncertainty intervals. The first interval
              crosses zero, so its direction is unknown. The second sits
              entirely above zero, so its direction is up.</title
            >
            <path d="M150 22 V132" class="k-line k-dash k-dim-st" />
            <text x="150" y="146" class="k-cap k-dim-fill">no effect</text>

            <text x="2" y="34" class="k-cap k-red-fill k-start">sign unknown</text>
            <path d="M60 50 H270" class="k-line k-red-st" />
            <path d="M60 42 V58" class="k-line k-red-st" />
            <path d="M270 42 V58" class="k-line k-red-st" />
            <circle cx="165" cy="50" r="5" class="k-red-fill" />

            <text x="2" y="94" class="k-cap k-teal-fill k-start">sign known: up</text>
            <path d="M178 110 H305" class="k-line k-teal-st" />
            <path d="M178 102 V118" class="k-line k-teal-st" />
            <path d="M305 102 V118" class="k-line k-teal-st" />
            <circle cx="232" cy="110" r="5" class="k-teal-fill" />
          </svg>
          <figcaption>
            Same question, two datasets. Only the second earns the word
            <em>directional</em>. Every value the data supports sits above zero.
            In the first, the data is equally consistent with the number rising
            and with it falling.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p class="dir-note">
          A line drawn through noisy points always slopes somewhere. Before
          calling it a trend, shuffle the dates and fit the line again. If it
          slopes about as steeply on the shuffled data, the slope is telling
          you nothing about time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 3.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">3.0 &nbsp;Term two</p>
        <h2>&ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t have to be perfect&rdquo; means nothing.</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing is perfect, including the number they are asking about. A
          quarter of revenue is itself an estimate, built on a close process and
          a set of accruals. The sentence rules out nothing. Any figure you hand
          over satisfies it, including the wrong ones.
        </p>
        <p>
          Machinists are never asked for perfect either. That is why every
          drawing carries a <strong>tolerance</strong>: 12.0 mm, &plusmn;0.2.
          The part is allowed to be wrong by a stated amount, in a stated
          direction, and the drawing says so before anyone cuts metal.
        </p>

        <figure class="dir-fig">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 340 155" role="img" aria-labelledby="dir-d2">
            <title id="dir-d2"
              >Two dimension lines. The first has a narrow tolerance band around
              its nominal value. The second is labeled plus or minus question
              mark and its band covers the whole line.</title
            >
            <text x="2" y="24" class="k-cap k-teal-fill k-start">12.0 &plusmn;0.2</text>
            <rect x="148" y="38" width="44" height="24" class="k-band k-teal-band" />
            <path d="M30 50 H310" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M30 40 V60" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M310 40 V60" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M170 32 V68" class="k-line k-teal-st" />

            <text x="2" y="104" class="k-cap k-red-fill k-start">12.0 &plusmn;?</text>
            <rect x="30" y="118" width="280" height="24" class="k-band k-red-band" />
            <path d="M30 130 H310" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M30 120 V140" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M310 120 V140" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
          </svg>
          <figcaption>
            The shaded band is what the spec permits. &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t
            have to be perfect&rdquo; is the second drawing. It permits every
            answer, which means it rules out none of them, and the width of the
            band ends up being whatever you quietly settled on.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 4.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card dir-card--turn">
        <p class="dir-num">4.0 &nbsp;The retort</p>
        <h2>One question puts the spec back in the room.</h2>
        <p class="dir-ask">
          &ldquo;Do you want something you can make the decision with?&rdquo;
          <span>ask it flatly, and wait</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          The answer is always yes, which is what makes the question worth
          asking. It moves the conversation off the deadline and onto how much
          error the decision can absorb. The follow-up gets you the number.
        </p>
        <p class="dir-ask dir-ask--alt">
          &ldquo;How wrong can this be before you&rsquo;d call it the other
          way?&rdquo;
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the number the drawing was missing. Write their answer down.
          From then on the tolerance is theirs, not an assumption you made alone
          at 11pm.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 5.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">5.0 &nbsp;The ladder</p>
        <h2>Decision-grade is a rung, not a mood.</h2>
        <p>
          Evidence comes in grades. Each grade carries a different weight of
          decision. Say which rung you are standing on.
        </p>

        <ol class="dir-ladder">
          <li class="rung rung--0">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R0</span> A coin toss</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              50/50, or a difference your sample cannot separate from zero.
              <strong>Carries nothing.</strong> They had this much before you
              opened the laptop.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rung rung--1">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R1</span> A correlation</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              Two things move together. <strong>Carries prediction, not
              intervention.</strong> Churn runs higher among accounts that never
              opened the mobile app, which is a fine reason to call them first.
              It is not a reason to believe that pushing them onto the app will
              keep them.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rung rung--2">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R2</span> A trend that beats noise</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              The movement survives shuffling, seasonality, and the obvious
              confounder. <strong>Carries a claim about direction.</strong> It
              carries nothing about size.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rung rung--3">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R3</span> A signed effect with an interval</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              The whole interval sits on one side of the number that would
              change their mind. <strong>Carries a go/no-go.</strong> This is
              the first rung worth the phrase <em>decision-grade</em>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rung rung--4">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R4</span> A magnitude with an interval</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              How big, how sure, against a stated threshold.
              <strong>Carries sizing:</strong> how much to spend, how many to
              hire, what to promise the board.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="dir-note">
          Most &ldquo;directional&rdquo; requests are answered at R1 and
          presented at R4. That gap is what shows up six months later, in the
          review of why the forecast missed.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 6.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">6.0 &nbsp;The spec</p>
        <h2>Four questions, two minutes, before any query runs.</h2>

        <ol class="dir-spec">
          <li>
            <span class="sp-q">Which way, or how much?</span>
            <span class="sp-d">Sign is cheap to establish. Magnitude needs a much larger sample, because you are asking the data to tell +3% from +8% rather than plus from minus. Pricing and headcount need magnitude. Kill-or-keep usually needs only the sign.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="sp-q">What number would change your mind?</span>
            <span class="sp-d">If no number would, the decision is already made, and your work is there to document it. Better to find that out on Tuesday than on Friday.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="sp-q">How wrong can it be before you&rsquo;d call it differently?</span>
            <span class="sp-d">The tolerance. Write it in the ticket, in their words, with their name next to it.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="sp-q">If we&rsquo;re wrong, when do we find out and what does the unwind cost?</span>
            <span class="sp-d">A cheap, reversible call deserves a fast, rough answer. Say that in the reply. Recommending less analysis than you could have done is how people learn to trust the times you ask for more.</span>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="dir-note">
          Run these four and the fast answer will often turn out to be enough.
          That is the good outcome. Ship it that afternoon, with the tolerance
          written down beside it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 7.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">7.0 &nbsp;The delivery</p>
        <h2>Hand back the number with its tolerance attached.</h2>
        <p>
          One sentence does it. Direction, range, the threshold it is being
          judged against, and the condition that would flip the call.
        </p>

        <figure class="dir-fig">
          <p class="dir-fig-h">The whole answer</p>
          <p class="dir-quote">
            &ldquo;Up. Best estimate +6%, and the data supports anywhere from
            +1% to +11%. Your bar is +2%. The low end clears it, but only just,
            and that is the part I would watch. Go. If the spend is above $200k,
            give me three more days and I&rsquo;ll tighten the range.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <figcaption>
            Twelve seconds to say. It gives the sign, the size, the
            uncertainty, the threshold, and what more certainty would cost.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p class="dir-note">
          Put it in writing the same day. A directional answer given in a
          meeting gets repeated a week later without the range. Written down,
          the range travels with it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ CLOSER ============ -->
      <section class="dir-close">
        <p class="dir-num">The job</p>
        <p class="dir-shout">Trade precision for speed, and put the trade in writing.</p>
        <p>
          Nobody is going to ask you for a tolerance. They will ask for
          directional, for not-perfect, and for it by lunchtime. You can give
          them all three. Name the tolerance first, and the lunchtime answer is
          one they can decide with.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ TITLE BLOCK ============ -->
      <footer class="dir-foot">
        <dl class="dir-block">
          <div><dt>Note</dt><dd>The directional-answer trap</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Field</dt><dd>Decision quality</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Drawn by</dt><dd>Alejandro Berrizbeitia</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Tolerance</dt><dd>Stated</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="dir-foot-h">Read these</p>
        <p>
          Judea Pearl and Dana Mackenzie, <em>The Book of Why</em> (2018). Why a
          correlation cannot answer a question about intervening, which is rung
          R1 above.
        </p>
        <p>
          Annie Duke, <em>How to Decide</em> (2020). Drills for naming the
          threshold before you look at the data.
        </p>
        <p>
          David Spiegelhalter, <em>The Art of Uncertainty</em> (2024). How to put
          a number on what you do not know, and how to say it to someone who is
          not a statistician.
        </p>
        <p class="dir-back">
          <a href="/field-notes/">← All field notes</a>
          <a href="/">aleberriz.com</a>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');

  /* Manila spec sheet. Scoped, so none of this reaches another note.
     `.dir` paints the full-bleed page, `.dir-sheet` is the drawing border,
     `.dir-inner` is the reading column. */
  .dir {
    --paper: #f2ede2;
    --sheet: #f8f5ee;
    --ink: #17171a;
    --body: #40444b;
    --dim: #6a6f78;
    --teal: #0a6a63;
    --red: #b4331f;
    --rule: rgba(23, 23, 26, 0.16);

    min-height: 100vh;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    background: var(--paper);
    color: var(--body);
    font-family: Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* Drawing sheet: double border, the way a drafting frame is ruled. */
  .dir-sheet {
    min-height: calc(100vh - 1.5rem);
    background: var(--sheet);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 4px var(--sheet), inset 0 0 0 5px var(--rule);
  }

  .dir-inner {
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 1.5rem 3.5rem;
  }

  .dir p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .dir strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .dir em { font-style: italic; }
  .dir a { color: var(--teal); }

  .dir-kicker,
  .dir-num,
  .dir-scroll,
  .dir-foot-h,
  .dir-fig-h,
  .rung-k,
  .dir-block dt {
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  /* ---- hero ---- */
  .dir-hero { padding: 3.5rem 0 3rem; }
  .dir-hero h1 {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 6.4vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1.22;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    text-wrap: balance;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .dir-hi {
    color: var(--teal);
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--teal);
  }
  .dir-strike {
    color: var(--red);
    text-decoration: line-through;
    text-decoration-thickness: 3px;
  }
  .dir-dek {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 34ch;
  }
  .dir-scroll { margin-top: 2.5rem; }

  /* ---- clauses ---- */
  .dir-card,
  .dir-close {
    padding: 2.75rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .dir-card h2 {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 1.25rem;
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 5.2vw, 1.75rem);
    line-height: 1.24;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .dir-card--turn h2 { color: var(--teal); }

  .dir-note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--red);
    font-size: 0.97rem;
  }

  /* ---- the two questions ---- */
  .dir-ask {
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    background: rgba(10, 106, 99, 0.07);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--teal);
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 4.2vw, 1.3rem);
    line-height: 1.35;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--teal);
  }
  .dir-ask span {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--dim);
  }
  .dir-ask--alt {
    background: none;
    border-left-style: dashed;
  }

  /* ---- figures ---- */
  .dir-fig { margin: 1.75rem 0; }
  .dir-fig svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .dir-fig figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--dim);
  }
  .dir-fig-h { margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }

  .k-line { fill: none; stroke-width: 1.6; }
  .k-ink-st { stroke: var(--ink); }
  .k-dim-st { stroke: var(--dim); }
  .k-red-st { stroke: var(--red); }
  .k-teal-st { stroke: var(--teal); }
  .k-dash { stroke-dasharray: 4 5; }
  .k-red-fill { fill: var(--red); }
  .k-teal-fill { fill: var(--teal); }
  .k-dim-fill { fill: var(--dim); }
  .k-band { stroke: none; }
  .k-teal-band { fill: rgba(10, 106, 99, 0.18); }
  .k-red-band { fill: rgba(180, 51, 31, 0.1); }
  .k-cap {
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  /* CSS beats the SVG presentation attribute, so set anchoring from classes. */
  .dir text.k-cap { text-anchor: middle; }
  .dir text.k-start { text-anchor: start; }

  /* ---- evidence ladder ---- */
  .dir-ladder { margin: 1.75rem 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .rung {
    position: relative;
    margin-left: 0.3rem;
    padding: 0.75rem 0 0.9rem 1.5rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .rung::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -6px;
    top: 1.15rem;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    background: #cdc7b9;
  }
  .rung:last-child { border-left-color: var(--teal); }
  .rung--3::before,
  .rung--4::before { background: var(--teal); }
  .rung--3, .rung--4 { border-left-color: var(--teal); }
  .rung--0::before { background: var(--red); }
  .rung-h {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .rung-k {
    margin-right: 0.55rem;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }
  .rung--0 .rung-k { color: var(--red); }
  .rung--3 .rung-k,
  .rung--4 .rung-k { color: var(--teal); }
  .rung-d { margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  /* ---- the four questions ---- */
  .dir-spec { margin: 1.5rem 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; counter-reset: sp; }
  .dir-spec li {
    padding: 0.9rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    counter-increment: sp;
  }
  .sp-q {
    display: block;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .sp-q::before {
    content: counter(sp, decimal-leading-zero) ' / ';
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--teal);
  }
  .sp-d { display: block; margin-top: 0.3rem; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  /* ---- the model answer ---- */
  .dir-quote {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem;
    background: rgba(23, 23, 26, 0.04);
    border: 1px dashed var(--rule);
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* ---- closer ---- */
  .dir-close { border-top: 3px solid var(--ink); }
  .dir-close .dir-num { color: var(--ink); }
  .dir-shout {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.16;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* ---- title block ---- */
  .dir-foot {
    padding: 2.5rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--dim);
  }
  .dir-block {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 0;
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    /* Only the top and left come from the container; each cell draws its own
       right and bottom, so the outer edge never doubles up. */
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-width: 1px 0 0 1px;
  }
  .dir-block > div {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .dir-block dt { margin: 0 0 0.2rem; }
  .dir-block dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .dir-back { margin-top: 1.75rem; display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .dir { padding: 1.25rem; }
    .dir-sheet { min-height: calc(100vh - 2.5rem); }
    .dir-inner { padding: 0 2.5rem 4rem; }
    .dir-block { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
  }

